(function () {
  var m = {"SVS334-R1": {"abstract": "Debugging distributed serverless applications used to mean stitching together X-Ray traces, CloudWatch logs, and custom metrics from multiple extensions. In this chalk talk, we cover setup and configuration, end-to-end tracing with API Gateway vended logs, and distributed tracing across Step Functions, EventBridge, and SQS. We also discuss observability for agentic workloads: tracking token consumption, tool call success rates, and workflow checkpoint state. Bring your observability questions, especially around cost at scale: sampling, filtering, and cardinality management.", "language": "en"},"SVS335-R": {"abstract": "What works at 10 Lambda functions may not work at 1,000. Deployments that took seconds now take hours. A single misconfigured function triggers account-level throttling. Observability drowns in noise. In this chalk talk, we discuss what changes at scale: deployment strategies that roll out code safely across hundreds of functions, account structure and concurrency management to prevent blast radius problems, observability approaches that surface signal without drowning in cardinality, and platform engineering patterns that let application teams ship independently. We share what large-scale Serverless operators do differently and where the common pitfalls are.", "language": "en"},"SVS335-R1": {"abstract": "What works at 10 Lambda functions may not work at 1,000. Deployments that took seconds now take hours. A single misconfigured function triggers account-level throttling. Observability drowns in noise. In this chalk talk, we discuss what changes at scale: deployment strategies that roll out code safely across hundreds of functions, account structure and concurrency management to prevent blast radius problems, observability approaches that surface signal without drowning in cardinality, and platform engineering patterns that let application teams ship independently. We share what large-scale Serverless operators do differently and where the common pitfalls are.", "language": "en"},"SVS336": {"abstract": "AI agents need more than an LLM and a prompt. Production agents require tool execution with least-privilege isolation, orchestration that recovers from partial failures, and observability that tracks token spend alongside function duration. In this chalk talk, we discuss architectural patterns for Serverless AI agents: model routing across providers and cost tiers, fault-tolerant orchestration with Step Functions and Lambda durable functions, isolated code execution using MicroVMs, and state management for long-running multi-step workflows. We whiteboard the decisions that matter for your workload shape and where each pattern introduces complexity you may not need.", "language": "en"},"SVS337": {"abstract": "Agents running generated code, multi-tenant platforms, and untrusted workloads all need strong isolation, but each traditional compute option forces a tradeoff. VMs isolate well but start slowly. Containers are fast but share a kernel. Functions scale to zero but limit state. Teams close the gaps with isolation logic they build and maintain themselves. Lambda MicroVMs offer sandboxes with VM-level isolation for each agent, tenant, or task: state that persists across invocations, automatic suspend on idle, resume on demand. This session covers how MicroVMs work, the security and isolation model, lifecycle management, and patterns for agentic sandboxing and untrusted code execution.", "language": "en"},"SVS338": {"abstract": "Serverless architectures that worked well for API backends and event processing now have a new type of consumer: AI agents that call your functions as tools, generate events, and write code that deploys alongside yours. In this session we cover where existing serverless patterns hold up without modification, what actually changes, and how to start with what you have and add only what you need. See how the calls you already run can become tools that describe themselves to the agent that uses them. Learn how coding agents and skills make best practices part of every build, and where doing less with AI pays off.", "language": "en"},"SVS339": {"abstract": "Your next API consumer might not be a person. AI agents, MCP servers, and coding assistants are calling APIs programmatically, and they need machine-readable schemas, predictable error contracts, and discoverable endpoints. In this session, we cover what's new with Amazon API Gateway: recent launches, direct service integrations that eliminate Lambda glue code, declarative event wiring, and patterns from real customer deployments. We also discuss how to design APIs that serve both human developers and autonomous agents without maintaining separate interfaces.", "language": "en"},"SVS341-R": {"abstract": "Most Lambda functions run at default memory settings, which means you're paying more for slower execution. Right-sizing memory often reduces cost and latency at the same time. In this chalk talk, we go beyond memory tuning: cold start elimination with SnapStart and provisioned concurrency, architecture-level savings like batching and right-sizing event source mappings, and when Lambda Managed Instances deliver better economics than per-invocation pricing. We whiteboard the decision points using real cost data and discuss which CloudWatch and Cost Explorer metrics actually drive optimization decisions.", "language": "en"},"SVS341-R1": {"abstract": "Most Lambda functions run at default memory settings, which means you're paying more for slower execution. Right-sizing memory often reduces cost and latency at the same time. In this chalk talk, we go beyond memory tuning: cold start elimination with SnapStart and provisioned concurrency, architecture-level savings like batching and right-sizing event source mappings, and when Lambda Managed Instances deliver better economics than per-invocation pricing. We whiteboard the decision points using real cost data and discuss which CloudWatch and Cost Explorer metrics actually drive optimization decisions.", "language": "en"},"SVS342-R": {"abstract": "Agents don't need new infrastructure - they need access to what you've already built. In this code talk, you'll retrofit a Serverless API into a production-ready agent tool, live in code. Implement an MCP server with versioned tools, wire it to AgentCore Gateway for discovery and invocation, then expose an entire Step Functions workflow, human-in-the-loop gates and all, as a single callable tool. Add scoped agent identity, watch an unauthorized call get blocked, then an authorized one go through. Walk away with a repeatable playbook for turning existing APIs into agent-ready tools.", "language": "en"},"SVS342-R1": {"abstract": "Agents don't need new infrastructure - they need access to what you've already built. In this code talk, you'll retrofit a Serverless API into a production-ready agent tool, live in code. Implement an MCP server with versioned tools, wire it to AgentCore Gateway for discovery and invocation, then expose an entire Step Functions workflow, human-in-the-loop gates and all, as a single callable tool. Add scoped agent identity, watch an unauthorized call get blocked, then an authorized one go through. Walk away with a repeatable playbook for turning existing APIs into agent-ready tools.", "language": "en"},"SVS343-R": {"abstract": "In this code talk, follow a Serverless application from first line of code to production deployment, all live. Start with an AI coding agent generating infrastructure and function scaffolding, iterate locally with hot reload, deploy in one command, then debug a failing request by tracing it across Lambda, EventBridge, and DynamoDB using native OpenTelemetry. Along the way, see how recent Serverless launches change the workflow: faster deploys, built-in observability, and simplified local testing. No slides, no diagrams. Just a terminal, an editor, and the workflow a Serverless developer uses in 2026.", "language": "en"},"SVS343-R1": {"abstract": "In this code talk, follow a Serverless application from first line of code to production deployment, all live. Start with an AI coding agent generating infrastructure and function scaffolding, iterate locally with hot reload, deploy in one command, then debug a failing request by tracing it across Lambda, EventBridge, and DynamoDB using native OpenTelemetry. Along the way, see how recent Serverless launches change the workflow: faster deploys, built-in observability, and simplified local testing. No slides, no diagrams. Just a terminal, an editor, and the workflow a Serverless developer uses in 2026.", "language": "en"},"SVS344": {"abstract": ".NET workloads on Lambda have historically meant slower cold starts and JIT warmup time that doesn't match Serverless execution patterns. Native AOT and SnapStart on .NET 10 close that gap, bringing cold starts under 200ms, but modernization isn't flip-a-switch. In this code talk, modernize a .NET function to AOT live, watch it fail, then fix it on stage: serialization with source generators, reflection-based DI replaced, trimming warnings resolved. See real measurements across AOT, SnapStart, and the managed runtime. Walk away with a library compatibility matrix and modernization patterns for your own .NET Serverless journey.", "language": "en"},"SVS347-R": {"abstract": "Modern web applications demand fast iteration, automatic scaling, and minimal operational overhead. In this code talk, build a full-stack web application entirely on AWS serverless services, live in code: API endpoints, business logic, data persistence, and authentication. We walk through architectural patterns for handling HTTP routing, server-side rendering, static assets, and real-time features without managing servers, and show how serverless simplifies common web framework patterns while delivering built-in high availability and pay-per-use economics. Whether you're migrating an existing web app or starting from scratch, you'll leave with patterns you can apply to your own projects.", "language": "en"},"SVS347-R1": {"abstract": "Modern web applications demand fast iteration, automatic scaling, and minimal operational overhead. In this code talk, build a full-stack web application entirely on AWS serverless services, live in code: API endpoints, business logic, data persistence, and authentication. We walk through architectural patterns for handling HTTP routing, server-side rendering, static assets, and real-time features without managing servers, and show how serverless simplifies common web framework patterns while delivering built-in high availability and pay-per-use economics. Whether you're migrating an existing web app or starting from scratch, you'll leave with patterns you can apply to your own projects.", "language": "en"},"SVS348": {"abstract": "Modern web applications demand fast iteration, automatic scaling, and minimal operational overhead. In this session, see how to build full-stack web applications entirely on AWS serverless services, from API endpoints and business logic to data persistence and authentication. We cover architectural patterns for handling HTTP routing, server-side rendering, static assets, and real-time features without managing servers, and how serverless simplifies common web framework patterns while delivering built-in high availability and pay-per-use economics. Whether you're migrating an existing web app or starting from scratch, you'll leave with practical blueprints for production-ready serverless web development.", "language": "en"},"SVS349": {"abstract": "Lambda's managed runtimes work until your platform team needs a pinned language version, a pre-loaded monitoring agent, or an SDK that every function must use. Today that means managing custom layers, coordinating updates across teams, and hoping nobody is running a version you deprecated last quarter. In this chalk talk, we whiteboard strategies for taking control of your Lambda runtime layer. Bring your runtime requirements and the constraints you're working around today.", "language": "en"},"SVS349-R": {"abstract": "Lambda's managed runtimes work until your platform team needs a pinned language version, a pre-loaded monitoring agent, or an SDK that every function must use. Today that means managing custom layers, coordinating updates across teams, and hoping nobody is running a version you deprecated last quarter. In this chalk talk, we whiteboard strategies for taking control of your Lambda runtime layer. Bring your runtime requirements and the constraints you're working around today.", "language": "en"},"SVS350": {"abstract": "If you're running Express, Hono, or Fastify on traditional serverless compute today, you're wrapping your HTTP server in an event-handler adapter and working around single-concurrency execution. In this session, see how AWS Lambda is evolving to support web workloads natively. Come see what's next for web developers on serverless.", "language": "en"},"SVS351": {"abstract": "AWS Lambda Managed Instances enables you to run Lambda functions on your choice of EC2 compute. With Lambda Managed Instances, you can optimize the performance and cost efficiency of your functions by utilizing specialized EC2 instance types and EC2 pricing benefits, while maintaining the familiar Serverless programming model and benefits like automatic patching, scaling, and 200+ AWS services integration for event-driven workloads. This session will provide an in-depth overview of how to get started with Lambda Managed Instances, recent launches like 90-minute function timeout, enhanced observability, and a behind-the-scenes look at how Lambda manages and scales your instances to ensure a seamless serverless experience.", "language": "en"},"SVS401-R": {"abstract": "AI agents are powerful, but production workloads need more than an LLM and a prompt. They require deterministic routing, audit trails, compensation logic, and human oversight. In this workshop, build agentic orchestration using AWS Lambda durable functions and MCP tools. Learn to design workflows that checkpoint progress, recover from failures automatically, and pause for human-in-the-loop approval—all without managing additional infrastructure. You'll implement tool-calling patterns, handle multi-step reasoning with retries, and deploy agents that are observable, testable, and production-ready. You must bring your laptop to participate.", "language": "en"},"SVS401-R1": {"abstract": "AI agents are powerful, but production workloads need more than an LLM and a prompt. They require deterministic routing, audit trails, compensation logic, and human oversight. In this workshop, build agentic orchestration using AWS Lambda durable functions and MCP tools. Learn to design workflows that checkpoint progress, recover from failures automatically, and pause for human-in-the-loop approval—all without managing additional infrastructure. You'll implement tool-calling patterns, handle multi-step reasoning with retries, and deploy agents that are observable, testable, and production-ready. You must bring your laptop to participate.", "language": "en"},"SVS402-R": {"abstract": "Modern web applications demand fast iteration, automatic scaling, and minimal operational overhead. In this session, discover how to build full-stack web applications entirely on AWS serverless services—from API endpoints and business logic to data persistence and authentication. Learn architectural patterns for handling HTTP routing, server-side rendering, static assets, and real-time features without managing servers. Explore how serverless simplifies common web framework patterns while delivering built-in high availability and pay-per-use economics. Whether you're migrating an existing web app or starting from scratch, you'll leave with practical blueprints for production-ready serverless web development.", "language": "en"},"SVS402-R1": {"abstract": "Modern web applications demand fast iteration, automatic scaling, and minimal operational overhead. In this session, discover how to build full-stack web applications entirely on AWS serverless services—from API endpoints and business logic to data persistence and authentication. Learn architectural patterns for handling HTTP routing, server-side rendering, static assets, and real-time features without managing servers. Explore how serverless simplifies common web framework patterns while delivering built-in high availability and pay-per-use economics. Whether you're migrating an existing web app or starting from scratch, you'll leave with practical blueprints for production-ready serverless web development.", "language": "en"},"SVS403-R": {"abstract": "AI workflows need state such as conversation history, intermediate results, and human approvals, but managing it shouldn't require a database and custom recovery logic. In this builders' session, build a stateful AI workflow that checkpoints progress, recovers from failures automatically, and pauses for external callbacks. Working hands-on with AWS experts, you'll implement multi-step reasoning chains with retry semantics, compare orchestration approaches for different workflow shapes, and deploy a working agent pipeline. Walk away with patterns for choosing the right orchestration tool for your workload. Bring your laptop.", "language": "en"},"SVS403-R1": {"abstract": "AI workflows need state such as conversation history, intermediate results, and human approvals, but managing it shouldn't require a database and custom recovery logic. In this builders' session, build a stateful AI workflow that checkpoints progress, recovers from failures automatically, and pauses for external callbacks. Working hands-on with AWS experts, you'll implement multi-step reasoning chains with retry semantics, compare orchestration approaches for different workflow shapes, and deploy a working agent pipeline. Walk away with patterns for choosing the right orchestration tool for your workload. Bring your laptop.", "language": "en"},"TNC101": {"abstract": "AWS Technical Essentials gives you the foundation to start building on AWS with confidence. In this bootcamp, navigate the AWS Management Console, configure IAM security controls, deploy compute workloads across EC2, Lambda, and containers, set up databases with RDS and DynamoDB, manage storage with S3, explore networking services, and monitor resources with CloudWatch. Hands-on labs reinforce each topic, so you leave ready to design available, scalable, and cost-effective solutions.", "language": "en"},"TNC102": {"abstract": "Turning an AI idea into enterprise reality requires more than technical knowledge. In this bootcamp, you apply the Discover, Design, Deliver framework to diagnose business problems, assess organizational readiness, and scope AI pilots with clear success criteria. You navigate responsible AI tradeoffs, lead change management across cross-functional teams, evaluate agentic AI architectures for autonomy versus control, and build phased scaling strategies from proof of concept through production deployment.", "language": "en"},"TNC103": {"abstract": "Replacement anxiety dominates the AI conversation, but builders who use AI as a daily collaborator are discovering the opposite: AI amplifies human capability. This breakout session presents a practical framework for using AI tooling as a skills accelerator. We'll show how builders who integrate AI into daily workflows develop stronger critical thinking, faster adaptability, and deeper domain reasoning. Drawing from real-world examples, you'll learn how the act of collaborating with AI and refining its direction builds the exact human capabilities organizations need most. Walk away with a progression model from proficiency to mastery and techniques to start sharpening your skills immediately.", "language": "en"},"TNC104": {"abstract": "Join this session to discover how AWS Training and Certification empowers partners to transform their workforce through strategic AI skills development. A partner shares their firsthand journey of designing AI learning pathways, implementing gamification that drove engagement at scale, and building sustainable certification programs tied to measurable business outcomes. You'll see how T&C enables this transformation, from foundational cloud fluency through advanced GenAI and Agentic AI readiness and leave with a concrete action plan to accelerate AI readiness across your teams.", "language": "en"},"TNC105": {"abstract": "Skills decay at unprecedented speed as AI reshapes what proficiency means. Certifications still validate expertise, but practitioners now need to demonstrate they can design, build, and optimize AI-powered systems alongside traditional cloud architecture. This lightning talk explains how AWS Training and Certification developed exam content, hands-on labs, and simulation-based assessments for agentic AI domains. You will also see how our team validated new competency frameworks, mapped learning paths to job roles, and built assessment experiences that measure real-world readiness rather than knowledge recall alone.", "language": "en"},"TNC106": {"abstract": "Lab Maker on AWS Skill Builder generates personalized hands-on labs from natural language prompts, allowing you to learn and experiment in the Console without incurring costs or risking production environments. During this session, we will generate a custom lab in real time before saving and sharing it with a team, demonstrating the full creation-to-collaboration workflow. We will then push Lab Maker further by generating a lab for a recently launched AWS service and uploading real organizational constraints to show how the tool adapts to your specific environment. You will leave this session with the knowledge and confidence to create your own labs.", "language": "en"},"TNC201-R": {"abstract": "The AI Development Lifecycle (AI-DLC) brings structure, traceability, and human oversight to every phase of high-velocity, Agentic-AI powered software delivery. In this bootcamp, apply AI-DLC in Kiro to build a production application through Feature Specs, control agent behavior with steering files and execution modes, extend capabilities with MCP servers and Kiro Powers, and automate workflows with hooks. Leave with a governed, repeatable process for shipping quality software with AI assistance.", "language": "en"},"TNC201-R1": {"abstract": "The AI Development Lifecycle (AI-DLC) brings structure, traceability, and human oversight to every phase of high-velocity, Agentic-AI powered software delivery. In this bootcamp, apply AI-DLC in Kiro to build a production application through Feature Specs, control agent behavior with steering files and execution modes, extend capabilities with MCP servers and Kiro Powers, and automate workflows with hooks. Leave with a governed, repeatable process for shipping quality software with AI assistance.", "language": "en"},"TNC208": {"abstract": "Security testing shouldn't require weeks of manual effort anymore. In this hands-on lab, configure and execute automated penetration tests using AWS Security Agent. Set up an Agent Space with VPC networking, verify target domains, scope tests by risk type, and monitor execution in real time. Analyze findings with CVSS scores and reproduction steps, then generate actionable reports for your development teams.", "language": "en"},"TNC209-R": {"abstract": "Amazon Quick brings AI-powered analytics, automation, and research into a single workspace. In this bootcamp, build interactive dashboards and visualizations with Quick Sight, organize team knowledge resources in Spaces with appropriate permissions, create custom Chat Agents to surface insights conversationally, automate repetitive workflows with Quick Flows, and conduct multi-source research with proper validation. The three hands-on labs give you practical experience to apply these skills immediately in your organization", "language": "en"},"TNC209-R1": {"abstract": "Amazon Quick brings AI-powered analytics, automation, and research into a single workspace. In this bootcamp, build interactive dashboards and visualizations with Quick Sight, organize team knowledge resources in Spaces with appropriate permissions, create custom Chat Agents to surface insights conversationally, automate repetitive workflows with Quick Flows, and conduct multi-source research with proper validation. The three hands-on labs give you practical experience to apply these skills immediately in your organization", "language": "en"},"TNC210-R": {"abstract": "This bootcamp is for AWS Partners only. Exploit a live AI agent with a prompt injection attack that approves a fraudulent transaction, then defend it. Fix it with Amazon Bedrock Guardrails to block prompt attacks and PII leaks. Verify it: prove your controls are in place with AWS Config conformance packs and find ungoverned AI resources with Resource Explorer. Observe it: trace agent decisions and guardrail interventions in Amazon Bedrock AgentCore. Leave ready to assess a customer's AI agents for governance gaps, protect them, and prove the controls work. You must bring your laptop to participate.", "language": "en"},"TNC210-R1": {"abstract": "This bootcamp is for AWS Partners only. Exploit a live AI agent with a prompt injection attack that approves a fraudulent transaction, then defend it. Fix it with Amazon Bedrock Guardrails to block prompt attacks and PII leaks. Verify it: prove your controls are in place with AWS Config conformance packs and find ungoverned AI resources with Resource Explorer. Observe it: trace agent decisions and guardrail interventions in Amazon Bedrock AgentCore. Leave ready to assess a customer's AI agents for governance gaps, protect them, and prove the controls work. You must bring your laptop to participate.", "language": "en"},"TNC211": {"abstract": "This bootcamp is for AWS Partners only. Walk through a hands-on business scenario using Amazon Quick, the agentic AI workspace that connects to your tools and learns your workflows. Create knowledge spaces, run deep-research agents, connect external APIs, automate multi-step processes with Flows, and build a custom AI agent in a browser. Leave able to help customers put agentic AI to work across their teams and business applications. You must bring your laptop to participate.", "language": "en"},"TNC212-R": {"abstract": "This bootcamp is for AWS Partners only. A good prompt gets you a chatbot. Everything else on AgentCore turns it into an agent. Build a customer support agent from scratch and progressively add persistent memory so it stops forgetting, tools through Gateway to reach real data, and Cedar policies that enforce boundaries, plus production traces, quality evaluations, and AI-driven optimization with A/B testing. Leave with hands-on experience on Amazon Bedrock AgentCore and ready to show customers what separates a chatbot from an agent worth paying for. You must bring your laptop to participate.", "language": "en"},"TNC212-R1": {"abstract": "This bootcamp is for AWS Partners only. A good prompt gets you a chatbot. Everything else on AgentCore turns it into an agent. Build a customer support agent from scratch and progressively add persistent memory so it stops forgetting, tools through Gateway to reach real data, and Cedar policies that enforce boundaries, plus production traces, quality evaluations, and AI-driven optimization with A/B testing. Leave with hands-on experience on Amazon Bedrock AgentCore and ready to show customers what separates a chatbot from an agent worth paying for. You must bring your laptop to participate.", "language": "en"},"TNC213-R": {"abstract": "Prepare for AWS Certified AI Practitioner by playing this 3D virtual escape room game where Certification prep meets competitive gaming. Generative AI knowledge unlocks doors here. Enter a narrative-driven 3D world, choose your avatar, and tackle progressively harder puzzles tied to AWS Certified AI Practitioner exam domains. Each locked room blends exam-style questions with hands-on labs covering foundation models, responsible AI, prompt engineering, and agentic workflows. Collaborate with teammates, solve challenges under time pressure, and race to escape before your peers to top the leaderboard.", "language": "en"},"TNC213-R1": {"abstract": "Prepare for AWS Certified AI Practitioner by playing this 3D virtual escape room game where Certification prep meets competitive gaming. Generative AI knowledge unlocks doors here. Enter a narrative-driven 3D world, choose your avatar, and tackle progressively harder puzzles tied to AWS Certified AI Practitioner exam domains. Each locked room blends exam-style questions with hands-on labs covering foundation models, responsible AI, prompt engineering, and agentic workflows. Collaborate with teammates, solve challenges under time pressure, and race to escape before your peers to top the leaderboard.", "language": "en"},"TNC214-R": {"abstract": "Prepare for AWS Certified Cloud Practitioner by playing this 3D virtual escape room game where Certification prep meets competitive gaming. Step into a 3D virtual world, choose your avatar, and race other players through rooms locked behind AWS Cloud Practitioner exam-style challenges. Each room combines interactive puzzles with hands-on labs that reinforce foundational cloud concepts like Compute, Storage, Networking, Security, and pricing models. Solve challenges collaboratively or compete solo, unlock rooms, and escape first to claim the leaderboard.", "language": "en"},"TNC214-R1": {"abstract": "Prepare for AWS Certified Cloud Practitioner by playing this 3D virtual escape room game where Certification prep meets competitive gaming. Step into a 3D virtual world, choose your avatar, and race other players through rooms locked behind AWS Cloud Practitioner exam-style challenges. Each room combines interactive puzzles with hands-on labs that reinforce foundational cloud concepts like Compute, Storage, Networking, Security, and pricing models. Solve challenges collaboratively or compete solo, unlock rooms, and escape first to claim the leaderboard.", "language": "en"},"TNC215": {"abstract": "Every minute of downtime is a minute your customers notice. What if an agent could correlate your logs, metrics, and change history the moment an application fails? In this hands-on lab, use AWS DevOps Agent to autonomously investigate operational issues across your AWS environment. Trigger investigations from simulated application failures, observe the agent correlate logs, metrics, and change history in real time, and review root cause analyses with actionable mitigation plans.", "language": "en"},"TNC216": {"abstract": "Your agent handles conversations well, but to search for flights or check weather it needs access to external tools and APIs. In this SimuLearn session, you create an Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Gateway and register Lambda functions as MCP-compatible tool targets. You then deploy a travel planning agent using the Strands Agents SDK that connects to the Gateway, sequentially invokes flight search and weather tools, and synthesizes results into a trip recommendation through one secure MCP endpoint.", "language": "en"},"TNC217": {"abstract": "AWS builds its certification exams, from foundational cloud to agentic AI, using production AI agents on Amazon Bedrock AgentCore. In this session, we show how we built our reasoning agents with AWS Well-Architected practices to solve real operational challenges: climbing latency, spiraling token costs, and non-deterministic failures. You’ll leave with concrete patterns to apply on AWS, plus a clear path to validate those skills through AWS Certification", "language": "en"},"TNC219": {"abstract": "Amazon Quick transforms how teams access insights and automate decisions. In this SimuLearn session, you design Spaces in Quick to organize enterprise knowledge, configure custom Chat Agents with specialized instructions and curated knowledge bases, and integrate Quick with enterprise systems using Actions and connectors backed by Amazon S3 or Amazon DynamoDB. You then apply row-level and column-level security controls to protect sensitive data and explore Quick Research for comprehensive multi-source analysis and reporting.", "language": "en"},"TNC220-R": {"abstract": "Go from zero to a deployed, observable AI agent in under an hour. Use the AgentCore CLI to scaffold a Strands agent with a custom tool, test it locally against the Runtime contract, then deploy to AgentCore Runtime via CDK. Invoke the agent to demonstrate session isolation, inspect automatic CloudWatch logs and X-Ray traces, and confirm the framework-agnostic contract by replacing the agent with a plain Python function.", "language": "en"},"TNC220-R1": {"abstract": "Go from zero to a deployed, observable AI agent in under an hour. Use the AgentCore CLI to scaffold a Strands agent with a custom tool, test it locally against the Runtime contract, then deploy to AgentCore Runtime via CDK. Invoke the agent to demonstrate session isolation, inspect automatic CloudWatch logs and X-Ray traces, and confirm the framework-agnostic contract by replacing the agent with a plain Python function.", "language": "en"},"TNC221-R": {"abstract": "A successful agent demo does not guarantee production readiness because agents can behave inconsistently across question types and multi-turn sessions. In this spotlight lab, you build two custom evaluators for an agent: THELMA, which scores single-turn RAG quality across seven dimensions including hallucination detection via groundedness scoring, and Mind the Goal, which computes Goal Success Rate across entire conversations and attributes failures to root causes. You register both as native AgentCore evaluators, run batch evaluations, and use diagnostic outputs to pinpoint exactly what to optimize first.", "language": "en"},"TNC221-R1": {"abstract": "A successful agent demo does not guarantee production readiness because agents can behave inconsistently across question types and multi-turn sessions. In this spotlight lab, you build two custom evaluators for an agent: THELMA, which scores single-turn RAG quality across seven dimensions including hallucination detection via groundedness scoring, and Mind the Goal, which computes Goal Success Rate across entire conversations and attributes failures to root causes. You register both as native AgentCore evaluators, run batch evaluations, and use diagnostic outputs to pinpoint exactly what to optimize first.", "language": "en"},"TNC302-R": {"abstract": "Application failures and security vulnerabilities demand fast answers to reduce downtime. In this bootcamp, learn how to setup AWS DevOps Agent and automate investigation failures through logs, metrics, and code changes, then generate remediation plans. Learn how to use AWS Security Agent to configure domain-scoped penetration tests, monitor real-time execution, and interpret severity-rated findings. Gain hands-on experience with two agents that compress hours of investigation into minutes.", "language": "en"},"TNC302-R1": {"abstract": "Application failures and security vulnerabilities demand fast answers to reduce downtime. In this bootcamp, learn how to setup AWS DevOps Agent and automate investigation failures through logs, metrics, and code changes, then generate remediation plans. Learn how to use AWS Security Agent to configure domain-scoped penetration tests, monitor real-time execution, and interpret severity-rated findings. Gain hands-on experience with two agents that compress hours of investigation into minutes.", "language": "en"},"TNC304": {"abstract": "This bootcamp is for AWS Partners only. AI workflows fail in ways that don't show up until production: a human misses the deadline, an external system says no, a sub-workflow loses state mid-flight. Fix it. Combine Step Functions, Lambda durable functions, and Amazon Bedrock AgentCore into a three-layer stack that routes decisions, checkpoints execution, and reasons through uncertainty. Run real scenarios through the Simulator Panel and trace every layer live. Leave ready to build AI workflows that laugh in the face of disruption. You must bring your laptop to participate.", "language": "en"},"TNC306-R": {"abstract": "Prepare for AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Associate by playing this 3D virtual escape room game. Enter a 3D virtual environment, select your avatar, and navigate rooms locked behind AWS Solutions Architect Associate challenges and design your way out. Build VPCs, select storage and database services, configure high availability, and implement fault-tolerant architectures while solving exam-aligned puzzles and completing hands-on labs. Each room builds on the previous, testing progressively deeper architectural knowledge. Collaborate with peers or compete solo and escape first to claim the leaderboard.", "language": "en"},"TNC306-R1": {"abstract": "Prepare for AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Associate by playing this 3D virtual escape room game. Enter a 3D virtual environment, select your avatar, and navigate rooms locked behind AWS Solutions Architect Associate challenges and design your way out. Build VPCs, select storage and database services, configure high availability, and implement fault-tolerant architectures while solving exam-aligned puzzles and completing hands-on labs. Each room builds on the previous, testing progressively deeper architectural knowledge. Collaborate with peers or compete solo and escape first to claim the leaderboard.", "language": "en"},"TNC307-R": {"abstract": "In this session, you will be given time to attempt a microcredential exam of your choice on AWS Skill Builder. These timed, hands-on exams test your skills in a live, provisioned AWS environment and test on popular topics. The product and developer team will be onsite to walk you through the UI and introduce the exam. Please note - this is an individual exam, and you will not be provided any hints or guidance outside of what is provided in the typical test environment. This exam is free to take on AWS Skill Builder. Attendees are required to bring their own device to complete the exam. Those who pass will be issued a microcredential badge on Credly.", "language": "en"},"TNC307-R1": {"abstract": "In this session, you will be given time to attempt a microcredential exam of your choice on AWS Skill Builder. These timed, hands-on exams test your skills in a live, provisioned AWS environment and test on popular topics. The product and developer team will be onsite to walk you through the UI and introduce the exam. Please note - this is an individual exam, and you will not be provided any hints or guidance outside of what is provided in the typical test environment. This exam is free to take on AWS Skill Builder. Attendees are required to bring their own device to complete the exam. Those who pass will be issued a microcredential badge on Credly.", "language": "en"},"TNC307-R2": {"abstract": "In this session, you will be given time to attempt a microcredential exam of your choice on AWS Skill Builder. These timed, hands-on exams test your skills in a live, provisioned AWS environment and test on popular topics. The product and developer team will be onsite to walk you through the UI and introduce the exam. Please note - this is an individual exam, and you will not be provided any hints or guidance outside of what is provided in the typical test environment. This exam is free to take on AWS Skill Builder. Attendees are required to bring their own device to complete the exam. Those who pass will be issued a microcredential badge on Credly.", "language": "en"},"TNC307-R3": {"abstract": "In this session, you will be given time to attempt a microcredential exam of your choice on AWS Skill Builder. These timed, hands-on exams test your skills in a live, provisioned AWS environment and test on popular topics. The product and developer team will be onsite to walk you through the UI and introduce the exam. Please note - this is an individual exam, and you will not be provided any hints or guidance outside of what is provided in the typical test environment. This exam is free to take on AWS Skill Builder. Attendees are required to bring their own device to complete the exam. Those who pass will be issued a microcredential badge on Credly.", "language": "en"},"TNC311-R": {"abstract": "Enhancing production apps requires balancing delivery velocity with code safety. Kiro gives you both. In this hands-on lab, add features to enhance a live application using AI-assisted development on Kiro. Define coding standards with steering files and specs, generate UI components from natural language prompts, automate quality checks with agent hooks, install powers for domain expertise, and delegate backend analysis to custom subagents. Deploy your changes confidently to production environments.", "language": "en"},"TNC311-R1": {"abstract": "Enhancing production apps requires balancing delivery velocity with code safety. Kiro gives you both. In this hands-on lab, add features to enhance a live application using AI-assisted development on Kiro. Define coding standards with steering files and specs, generate UI components from natural language prompts, automate quality checks with agent hooks, install powers for domain expertise, and delegate backend analysis to custom subagents. Deploy your changes confidently to production environments.", "language": "en"},"TNC312-R": {"abstract": "When your data changes frequently, RAG helps your agent access the latest information at query time. Fine-tuning an LLM with domain data brings precision in how the agent responds and executes. In this hands-on lab, build reliable AI agents by fine-tuning an LLM on Amazon SageMaker AI for valid tool-call output and consistent persona, and connect it to Amazon Bedrock RAG to ground responses in your latest policies and customer data.", "language": "en"},"TNC312-R1": {"abstract": "When your data changes frequently, RAG helps your agent access the latest information at query time. Fine-tuning an LLM with domain data brings precision in how the agent responds and executes. In this hands-on lab, build reliable AI agents by fine-tuning an LLM on Amazon SageMaker AI for valid tool-call output and consistent persona, and connect it to Amazon Bedrock RAG to ground responses in your latest policies and customer data.", "language": "en"},"TNC315-R": {"abstract": "Legacy .NET Framework applications tied to Windows limit your deployment options and increase operational costs. In this lab, you use AWS Transform for .NET in Interactive Mode to automatically port a .NET Framework 4.8 Web Forms application to .NET 10 Blazor application. You review the AWS Transform generated assessment report and migration plan, accept the automated transformation, then use Kiro to identify and fix the residual runtime issue that remains after the port.", "language": "en"},"TNC315-R1": {"abstract": "Legacy .NET Framework applications tied to Windows limit your deployment options and increase operational costs. In this lab, you use AWS Transform for .NET in Interactive Mode to automatically port a .NET Framework 4.8 Web Forms application to .NET 10 Blazor application. You review the AWS Transform generated assessment report and migration plan, accept the automated transformation, then use Kiro to identify and fix the residual runtime issue that remains after the port.", "language": "en"},"TNC317": {"abstract": "Customers expect personalized service and context retention without repeating themselves every session. In this lab, you build a customer service agent on Amazon Bedrock AgentCore that remembers each client across conversations. You create a Memory resource with Semantic, Summary, and User Preference strategies, deploy a Lambda-based agent backed by Amazon Nova Lite, and seed multi-turn conversations. The agent retrieves preferences and facts from long-term memory to personalize responses and recommend products from a DynamoDB catalog.", "language": "en"},"TNC318-R": {"abstract": "You need more than one agent to reason across inventory, shipments, forecasts, and suppliers at the same time. In this lab, you deploy a multi-agent supply chain system on Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Runtime. You load data into DynamoDB, build container images with CodeBuild, and deploy specialist agents plus a supervisor orchestrator powered by Amazon Nova models. The orchestrator classifies questions, routes them to the right specialists in parallel, and synthesizes their responses into one coherent answer.", "language": "en"},"TNC318-R1": {"abstract": "You need more than one agent to reason across inventory, shipments, forecasts, and suppliers at the same time. In this lab, you deploy a multi-agent supply chain system on Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Runtime. You load data into DynamoDB, build container images with CodeBuild, and deploy specialist agents plus a supervisor orchestrator powered by Amazon Nova models. The orchestrator classifies questions, routes them to the right specialists in parallel, and synthesizes their responses into one coherent answer.", "language": "en"},"TNC322-R": {"abstract": "In this SimuLearn session, you leverage the entire Amazon Bedrock AgentCore platform ito build production-grade wealth management advisor. You create Memory resources for client personalization, configure a Gateway with MCP tool targets for account and market data, deploy specialist agents and a supervisor to Runtime, and authenticate users through Cognito. The result is a multi-agent advisor that recognizes each client, remembers their goals, and produces auditable financial guidance through governed tools.", "language": "en"},"TNC322-R1": {"abstract": "In this SimuLearn session, you leverage the entire Amazon Bedrock AgentCore platform ito build production-grade wealth management advisor. You create Memory resources for client personalization, configure a Gateway with MCP tool targets for account and market data, deploy specialist agents and a supervisor to Runtime, and authenticate users through Cognito. The result is a multi-agent advisor that recognizes each client, remembers their goals, and produces auditable financial guidance through governed tools.", "language": "en"},"TNC323-R": {"abstract": "In a simulated scenario, Fitness trainers waste 30 minutes daily on manual data entry and cannot spot trends across clients. In this SimuLearn session, you build FitTrack AI using Kiro's spec-driven workflows and Amazon Bedrock AgentCore. You design flexible schemas, create agent tools for client management and natural language analytics, deploy the agent on ECS Fargate, configure Bedrock Guardrails for health safety, and implement anomaly detection using database change streams.", "language": "en"},"TNC323-R1": {"abstract": "In a simulated scenario, Fitness trainers waste 30 minutes daily on manual data entry and cannot spot trends across clients. In this SimuLearn session, you build FitTrack AI using Kiro's spec-driven workflows and Amazon Bedrock AgentCore. You design flexible schemas, create agent tools for client management and natural language analytics, deploy the agent on ECS Fargate, configure Bedrock Guardrails for health safety, and implement anomaly detection using database change streams.", "language": "en"},"TNC324-R": {"abstract": "Customer-facing AI Agents can resolve issues end-to-end. In this bootcamp, build two complementary AI agents on Amazon Connect AI Customer. First, create an agent-assistance copilot that watches live chats and surfaces real-time knowledge base answers and customer data lookups to human agents. Then build a self-service AI agent that handles routine customer requests autonomously, using MCP tools for Cases and Customer Profiles, and structured escalation paths that preserve full conversation context when a human is needed.", "language": "en"},"TNC324-R1": {"abstract": "Customer-facing AI Agents can resolve issues end-to-end. In this bootcamp, build two complementary AI agents on Amazon Connect AI Customer. First, create an agent-assistance copilot that watches live chats and surfaces real-time knowledge base answers and customer data lookups to human agents. Then build a self-service AI agent that handles routine customer requests autonomously, using MCP tools for Cases and Customer Profiles, and structured escalation paths that preserve full conversation context when a human is needed.", "language": "en"},"TNC325-R": {"abstract": "Multi-agent systems unlock powerful workflows, but production demands more than chaining agents together. This bootcamp covers advanced patterns using Amazon Bedrock AgentCore. Implement agent-as-tool communication, share memory across agents, optimize context windows for cost and performance, and secure deployments with policy-based access control and VPC integration. Configure distributed tracing, build evaluation frameworks, and design deployment strategies that take your agentic systems from prototype to production-ready state.", "language": "en"},"TNC325-R1": {"abstract": "Multi-agent systems unlock powerful workflows, but production demands more than chaining agents together. This bootcamp covers advanced patterns using Amazon Bedrock AgentCore. Implement agent-as-tool communication, share memory across agents, optimize context windows for cost and performance, and secure deployments with policy-based access control and VPC integration. Configure distributed tracing, build evaluation frameworks, and design deployment strategies that take your agentic systems from prototype to production-ready state.", "language": "en"},"TNC326-R": {"abstract": "This bootcamp is for AWS Partners only. AI agents work great in demos but break in production. Replace luck with observability. Prototype a role-based agent with the Strands SDK. Evaluate it with custom evaluators for quality and compliance. Deploy it to production on Amazon Bedrock AgentCore. Observe it with OpenTelemetry tracing and CloudWatch dashboards. Detect drift by evaluating production traffic at scale. Leave ready to take a customer's AI agent from prototype to production-grade. You must bring your laptop to participate.", "language": "en"},"TNC326-R1": {"abstract": "This bootcamp is for AWS Partners only. AI agents work great in demos but break in production. Replace luck with observability. Prototype a role-based agent with the Strands SDK. Evaluate it with custom evaluators for quality and compliance. Deploy it to production on Amazon Bedrock AgentCore. Observe it with OpenTelemetry tracing and CloudWatch dashboards. Detect drift by evaluating production traffic at scale. Leave ready to take a customer's AI agent from prototype to production-grade. You must bring your laptop to participate.", "language": "en"},"TNC328": {"abstract": "Customers expect instant answers about their orders, accounts, and open cases. In this hands-on lab, build a self-service AI agent in Amazon Connect that autonomously resolves customer requests by looking up profiles, searching and creating cases through MCP tools, and retrieving FAQ answers from a knowledge base. Configure AI Guardrails for content safety and structured escalation paths that preserve full conversation context for human agents.", "language": "en"},"TNC329-R": {"abstract": "Your agent may handle basic conversations well, but to take real-world actions it needs access to external tools and APIs. In this hands-on lab, use Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Gateway to expose Lambda functions as MCP-compatible tools with JWT authorization. Register tool targets, connect to a pre-deployed nutrition coach agent, and validate how it dynamically discovers and invokes new capabilities at runtime without redeployment.", "language": "en"},"TNC329-R1": {"abstract": "Your agent may handle basic conversations well, but to take real-world actions it needs access to external tools and APIs. In this hands-on lab, use Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Gateway to expose Lambda functions as MCP-compatible tools with JWT authorization. Register tool targets, connect to a pre-deployed nutrition coach agent, and validate how it dynamically discovers and invokes new capabilities at runtime without redeployment.", "language": "en"},"TNC330": {"abstract": "A personal finance assistant agent deployed on AgentCore Runtime starts showing intermittent failures and slow responses. In this lab, you will use AgentCore Observability with CloudWatch GenAI dashboards and Transaction Search to diagnose production issues. Trace errors through spans, correlate telemetry with application logs, and identify root causes including a tool timeout and conversation history bloat. Apply fixes, redeploy to AgentCore Runtime, and set up CloudWatch Alarms to catch issues before users do.", "language": "en"},"TNC331-R": {"abstract": "Building a production-ready AI agent meant writing orchestration code, packaging containers, and managing deployment pipelines. AgentCore Harness eliminates all of that. In this hands-on lab, you declare what your agent does through configuration and let AgentCore handle the execution loop. You create a harness agent, iteratively add tools, attach AWS Skills, swap models mid-session to suit your output requirements, and publish versioned endpoints for safe rollout to your customers.", "language": "en"},"TNC331-R1": {"abstract": "Building a production-ready AI agent meant writing orchestration code, packaging containers, and managing deployment pipelines. AgentCore Harness eliminates all of that. In this hands-on lab, you declare what your agent does through configuration and let AgentCore handle the execution loop. You create a harness agent, iteratively add tools, attach AWS Skills, swap models mid-session to suit your output requirements, and publish versioned endpoints for safe rollout to your customers.", "language": "en"},"TNC332": {"abstract": "Not every caller should have access to your AI agents, and you should verify who is calling them before responding. In this hands-on lab, you deploy an IT help desk agent to Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Runtime and configure AgentCore Identity to enforce inbound authentication. You set up an Amazon Cognito user pool as the identity provider, obtain and decode a JSON Web Token (JWT), and test that the agent rejects unauthenticated callers while allowing verified employees through.", "language": "en"},"TNC333-R": {"abstract": "Kiro, the Agentic IDE, transforms a single natural language prompt into structured requirements, a technical design, and an executable implementation plan. In this SimuLearn session, you build a city event management application by guiding Kiro through its spec-driven workflow: generating EARS-format user stories, reviewing architecture decisions, and executing a sequenced task list that produces a working MVP. You also configure a Kiro Hook that automatically validates data files on save.", "language": "en"},"TNC333-R1": {"abstract": "Kiro, the Agentic IDE, transforms a single natural language prompt into structured requirements, a technical design, and an executable implementation plan. In this SimuLearn session, you build a city event management application by guiding Kiro through its spec-driven workflow: generating EARS-format user stories, reviewing architecture decisions, and executing a sequenced task list that produces a working MVP. You also configure a Kiro Hook that automatically validates data files on save.", "language": "en"},"TNC334-R": {"abstract": "Claims adjusters spend hours retrieving policy data, applying business rules, and drafting decision letters manually. In this SimuLearn session, you use Amazon Quick Automate to orchestrate the entire claims process. You configure REST API and MCP connectors, build a multi-step workflow with Custom Agent steps, and chain together policy retrieval, rules validation, and AI-powered letter generation. The workflow handles missing data gracefully and stores audit-ready decision letters in S3.", "language": "en"},"TNC334-R1": {"abstract": "Claims adjusters spend hours retrieving policy data, applying business rules, and drafting decision letters manually. In this SimuLearn session, you use Amazon Quick Automate to orchestrate the entire claims process. You configure REST API and MCP connectors, build a multi-step workflow with Custom Agent steps, and chain together policy retrieval, rules validation, and AI-powered letter generation. The workflow handles missing data gracefully and stores audit-ready decision letters in S3.", "language": "en"},"TNC335-R": {"abstract": "A customer runs a support agent on Amazon Bedrock AgentCore that reaches destinations nobody reviewed and takes requests from any caller. In this SimuLearn session, you work the case as their security expert. Behind Amazon API Gateway, AWS WAF Bot Control labels each script and AI crawler, and you write the rule that refuses them with a 403 that names the reason. On the way out, AWS Network Firewall tags every destination by content category. You watch in alert mode, then reject AI and search categories while permitted sites still load. You leave knowing how to classify, then control, what enters and leaves an agent.", "language": "en"},"TNC335-R1": {"abstract": "AI agents decide at runtime which endpoints to call, and that decision can bypass your security review. In this SimuLearn session, you deploy an agent on Amazon Bedrock AgentCore in VPC mode and route its outbound traffic through AWS Network Firewall. You configure a default-deny policy with an approved-domain allow list, then prove it works: an approved call succeeds while an unapproved call gets dropped. The firewall log becomes your audit evidence.", "language": "en"},"TNC336-R": {"abstract": "The AI Development Lifecycle (AI-DLC) reimagines how teams plan, build, and ship software with AI as a first-class participant. In this bootcamp, learn the three phases (Inception, Construction, Operation) , apply the Intent-Unit-Bolt framework to decompose requirements into AI-executable work items, then use Kiro IDE's Spec-Driven Development to generate domain models, application code, tests, and infrastructure. You leave with a working application built entirely through the AI-DLC methodology.", "language": "en"},"TNC336-R1": {"abstract": "The AI Development Lifecycle (AI-DLC) reimagines how teams plan, build, and ship software with AI as a first-class participant. In this bootcamp, learn the three phases (Inception, Construction, Operation) , apply the Intent-Unit-Bolt framework to decompose requirements into AI-executable work items, then use Kiro IDE's Spec-Driven Development to generate domain models, application code, tests, and infrastructure. You leave with a working application built entirely through the AI-DLC methodology.", "language": "en"},"TNC337": {"abstract": "AWS Transform orchestrates AI agents that analyze, refactor, and convert legacy applications without manual line-by-line rewrites. In this bootcamp, set modernization objectives, let AI agents decompose and transform .NET Framework code to cross-platform .NET on Linux, then migrate a SQL Server database to Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL. Practice agentic assessment, wave planning, code transformation, and schema conversion end to end.", "language": "en"},"TNC338": {"abstract": "Production infrastructure needs two guarantees: repeatable deployment and proven resilience. In this bootcamp, use AWS CDK and CloudFormation to define and provision cloud resources as code, eliminating manual configuration drift. Then validate your architecture under stress. Troubleshoot VPC connectivity using flow logs, inject controlled faults with AWS Fault Injection Service, and confirm your infrastructure recovers gracefully. Deploy it right, then prove it holds.", "language": "en"},"TNC339-R": {"abstract": "This bootcamp is for AWS Partners only. An agent is only as reliable as the services it calls. Before you fix anything, you need to know exactly what's broken. In this workshop you stress-test that claim — probing a service for gaps that break autonomous invocation, reading the findings against your own predictions, and making the call on what gets fixed first. Leave with a ranked fix list and a method that transfers to any service. You must bring your laptop to participate.", "language": "en"},"TNC339-R1": {"abstract": "This bootcamp is for AWS Partners only. An agent is only as reliable as the services it calls. Before you fix anything, you need to know exactly what's broken. In this workshop you stress-test that claim — probing a service for gaps that break autonomous invocation, reading the findings against your own predictions, and making the call on what gets fixed first. Leave with a ranked fix list and a method that transfers to any service. You must bring your laptop to participate.", "language": "en"},"TNC340-R": {"abstract": "AWS DevOps Agent investigates production incidents end-to-end: tracing failures to the responsible change, guiding the fix, and verifying recovery. Once the service is stable, the agent converts root cause findings into automated prevention rules that enforce mandatory review gates on the same class of risk in future deployments. In this SimuLearn session, experience the full investigate, resolve, and prevent lifecycle as one continuous workflow in a single session.", "language": "en"},"TNC340-R1": {"abstract": "AWS DevOps Agent investigates production incidents end-to-end: tracing failures to the responsible change, guiding the fix, and verifying recovery. Once the service is stable, the agent converts root cause findings into automated prevention rules that enforce mandatory review gates on the same class of risk in future deployments. In this SimuLearn session, experience the full investigate, resolve, and prevent lifecycle as one continuous workflow in a single session.", "language": "en"},"WOC101": {"abstract": "The traditional playbook for executive leadership is undergoing fundamental change as hybrid ecosystems of human employees and autonomous AI agents become commonplace, with agents acting as co-workers, specialists, and decision-makers. This shift requires executives to expand their skill set, dividing organizational goals between human creative strategy and agentic operational execution. Moving past the AI hype, this session examines how human roles must evolve from executors to context engineers and decision architects, and how career progression must be rewritten when entry-level and repetitive work is owned by digital labor. Explore how women in leadership will adapt and build tomorrow's leaders.", "language": "en"},"rec3n5lvp9BT9wyTK": {"abstract": "KOHO is pursuing a Canadian banking license with AI central to its operations. Every team uses Claude through Amazon Bedrock, and its first financial assistant, powered by Amazon Bedrock AgentCore, is live in beta with 5,000 customers. One engineer built it in two weeks. This session examines how a small platform team governs model access across the company and helps teams deploy agents that handle financial data. KOHO shares its approach to keeping data in controlled environments, building human oversight and fallbacks into automated workflows, and providing shared infrastructure so teams can deploy agents with consistent safeguards and operational visibility.", "language": "en"},"rec4WOTlcBiZJvbAz": {"abstract": "Vega Security’s AI-native Security Analytics Mesh runs threat detection directly on customer data in Amazon S3, avoiding costly ingestion and migration. Co-founder Eli Rozen explains the architecture behind the platform and how Vega reduced mean triage time from 25 minutes to under eight minutes and false positives by 48% for Fortune 500 customers. The session examines how Vega uses Amazon Bedrock and AWS Graviton for production AI inference, with AWS consumption reaching $2.3 million and growing 880% year over year. Security leaders and builders will explore the design choices behind detection on customer data and running untrusted AI workloads safely.", "language": "en"},"rec4e46eINvocCYth": {"abstract": "Eligible attendees can collect their exclusive conference swag at designated pickup locations in Hall C of the Venetian Convention Center. Attendees must present their valid conference badge to receive their items. Swag availability is limited and distributed on a first-come, first-served basis during official event hours. Note that eligibility for items may depend on your attendee pass type.", "language": "en"},"rec5HhOs4FQwIxUGu": {"abstract": "Build your strategy and your cloud skills with BuilderCards- a fun, interactive deck-building game challenging you to connect AWS services and architect scalable solutions. Come share laughs, strategize together, and engage in a friendly competition, no matter where you are on your cloud journey.", "language": "en"},"rec5wqOI4qbCHOUDM": {"abstract": "Eligible attendees can collect their exclusive conference swag at designated pickup locations in Hall C of the Venetian Convention Center. Attendees must present their valid conference badge to receive their items. Swag availability is limited and distributed on a first-come, first-served basis during official event hours. Note that eligibility for items may depend on your attendee pass type.", "language": "en"},"rec6McYM4WwJ1bqDL": {"abstract": "Retell powers voice agents for enterprise contact centers, where a split-second delay can end a call. In three years, Retell grew from a viral launch to $100M ARR and 100M+ calls per month on AWS, with traffic doubling every few months and 100x swings between day and night, while holding >99.9% uptime without ever re-platforming. CTO Zexia Zhang traces how Retell's stack evolved: ECS and DynamoDB at launch, ElastiCache and Lambda and SQS for spiky load, and EKS for global expansion. Builders of real-time AI will learn how to scale GPU-intensive workloads, contain latency across services, and stay reliable as usage grows. Speaker: Zexia Zhang, Co-founder & CTO, Retell AI.", "language": "en"},"rec6SzheLUuAsR1iL": {"abstract": "The first 90 days after an enterprise contract can determine whether an AI deployment earns renewal or stalls. Forward Deployed Engineering places engineers inside customer teams to own production code and business outcomes. This session explains when startups need FDE and how it differs from solutions engineering and customer success. An interactive scenario follows one startup’s deployment into its second year. The room decides how to scope the work, handle a security review, respond to a one-off feature request, and hire the first FDE. We examine the consequences, including which customer-specific work becomes reusable product and when embedded engineering pays.", "language": "en"},"rec6Xbk8lsOOli82K": {"abstract": "Each week, over 10,000 cancer patients wait for a clinical trial match, but delayed chart reviews can prevent timely identification. Triomics uses Amazon Bedrock to process patient records at 10 million tokens per minute, reducing weeks of review to hours. Bedrock initially appeared 67% more expensive than GCP for its primary model. By matching Standard, Flex, and Batch tiers to workload patterns, Triomics achieved 25% net cost savings while preserving vendor flexibility. Hrituraj Singh, co-founder and CTO, explains the cost analysis and validation behind migrating off Azure, showing how workload-based pricing supports faster clinical review while controlling inference costs and preserving model choice.", "language": "en"},"rec8HYMD2egUR83Ht": {"abstract": "Wonderful builds enterprise voice agents for regulated markets where English-first platforms struggle with local speech patterns and compliance requirements. Its platform handles conversations in more than 30 countries, supported by locally embedded teams. CEO Bar Winkler explains the Forward Deployed Engineer model, with senior engineers working inside customer environments and owning delivery through deployment. He shares how Wonderful deployed for a major telecom provider in two weeks, compared with the months typically required. The session examines how local expertise shapes agent performance, how to staff engineering teams across regions, and what technical accountability requires when deploying voice AI in customer environments.", "language": "en"},"rec9cKEsfpO31RsWn": {"abstract": "Making humanoid and autonomous robots useful requires more than a capable chassis. Leaders from NEURA Robotics, 1X, and Highlanders discuss the intelligence behind their robots, in a panel moderated by Deap Ubhi. The conversation examines how teams train and simulate robot behaviors, which AI models they use, and how data pipelines support development. Panelists also explore the application layer that turns robotic capabilities into products customers can use. Startup founders and robotics builders will hear how these teams connect training and simulation with application development as they bring robots out of the lab and into real-world use by customers. **", "language": "en"},"recAkxo4C8eAZ2iSx": {"abstract": "Processing 2,500 legal cases daily with a large general-purpose model cost Dharma-AI R$1 million per month. Replacing its 23-prompt chain with a smaller, fine-tuned open model cut monthly costs to R$5,000, while accuracy decreased from 88% to 86%. CTO Elisa Mussumeci explains how Dharma-AI achieved this tradeoff and built infrastructure to support model specialization. She covers Karmada for federating Amazon EKS clusters across regions, EC2 Spot instances for cheaper compute, and Karpenter for GPU provisioning. The session also examines automated training-recipe selection and how to compare fine-tuning with prompting when deciding which approach meets a production workload's cost and accuracy requirements.", "language": "en"},"recCJS9bz3B6bjHxB": {"abstract": "Build your strategy and your cloud skills with BuilderCards- a fun, interactive deck-building game challenging you to connect AWS services and architect scalable solutions. Come share laughs, strategize together, and engage in a friendly competition, no matter where you are on your cloud journey.", "language": "en"},"recCsq6gGZYJQ4lXT": {"abstract": "With Supabase, developers can use AI agents to build application backends in minutes instead of weeks. Adoption grew from 1,500 databases launched daily in early 2024 to more than one million in a single week in Q3 2026. Running entirely on AWS, Supabase uses Amazon S3 and Amazon EC2 instances with AWS Graviton processors alongside Amazon EKS and Amazon CloudWatch to support that growth. This session shows how startups can use Supabase to launch AI applications quickly and examines the infrastructure behind its database provisioning at scale. Builders of developer tools and AI applications will explore how simpler backend setup reduces the time needed to test ideas.", "language": "en"},"recDdBkIrbIZWSyX4": {"abstract": "Halter, a $2.86 billion New Zealand agricultural tech company backed by Peter Thiel, uses autonomous AI agents across internal operations and its farmer-facing product. Clank, built on Claude through Amazon Bedrock, runs over 171 concurrent agents and processes 15.8 million MCP invocations weekly, saving up to 215 manual engineering hours in three months. Mav, built on Amazon Bedrock AgentCore, is rolling out to over 2,000 farms across New Zealand, Australia, and the US. Antony Southworth explains the architecture behind both systems, how Halter handles failures and monitors distributed workloads, and the operational demands of running agents across teams and customer products.", "language": "en"},"recDrtmfwILRnIwhv": {"abstract": "Manifold Bio designs millions of protein molecules each month for drug discovery, but GPU capacity across US regions could not meet demand. Using the open-source AWS Global Capacity Orchestrator, the company accessed compute worldwide without rebuilding its pipelines and scaled workloads fourfold. Erik Swanson, AI/ML Researcher, explains how multi-region orchestration supports these workloads, with Amazon EC2 Spot instances and Amazon EKS providing elastic compute while controlling costs. This session examines the decisions behind expanding beyond regional capacity limits and coordinating distributed resources. The architecture also applies to machine learning and simulation workloads that need more compute capacity than individual regions can provide.", "language": "en"},"recHo4rlA0R4SFPMT": {"abstract": "Interactive world models must process user input and stream the next frame in under 40 milliseconds. Serving 10,000 concurrent sessions makes infrastructure a bottleneck. Builders and operators explain how they keep state in GPU memory while maintaining utilization, manage cold-start spikes, and plan capacity around session economics. The discussion examines how GPUs, Trainium, and other accelerators fit different workloads and affect latency and cost. Media and entertainment teams will hear where current approaches fail and what breaks as concurrent sessions grow tenfold or hundredfold, grounding production infrastructure decisions in the demands of delivering interactive experiences reliably to many users at once.", "language": "en"},"recJmpS7MWrCfzP8w": {"abstract": "Cartesia built its voice inference stack in-house to deliver responses in under 250 milliseconds across millions of interactions. Running on AWS Trainium, the stack supports real-time voice generation where delays disrupt conversation. Cartesia ranks first on Artificial Analysis for text-to-speech quality, latency, and cost. This session examines when custom inference infrastructure makes sense compared with managed endpoints and how to benchmark performance across chips. The team explains the engineering decisions behind its stack and work with AWS silicon teams. A live demo shows Cartesia’s voice models running on Trainium, connecting infrastructure choices to response speed and the cost of inference.", "language": "en"},"recKNtoOktTIN4iup": {"abstract": "Aily Labs built an AI-native Decision Intelligence platform orchestrating AI agents across finance, supply chain, manufacturing, R&D, and commercial functions—all powered by a 'Super Agent' running on multiple foundation models via Amazon Bedrock. The hook: enterprise customers can deploy these agents inside their own AWS environment in as little as one day, with full governance, security, and observability. Aily Labs solves a real enterprise problem: companies are sitting on massive operational data across functions but struggle to turn that data into timely decisions. This session is critical for any founder building enterprise AI orchestration, anyone thinking about how agents change the shape of enterprise software, and sales leaders selling to large organizations.", "language": "en"},"recLAA0kBvTQNWsnE": {"abstract": "Market research can be slow and expensive. Simile, a Stanford spinout, builds agentic twins: AI stand-ins trained on in-depth interviews with hundreds of people. Companies use these models to simulate consumer decisions instead of conducting a new survey for every question. Customers include CVS Health and Gallup. Joon Park, CEO and co-founder, explains how Simile collects interview data and turns it into behavioral models, then develops products for enterprises seeking better consumer understanding. The session examines what it takes to build and sell AI simulations for market research, with a focus on using interview-based models to explore consumer choices and test research questions. **VC Lightning Talks**", "language": "en"},"recNEdPG2OWRGkQmr": {"abstract": "Forlex shipped a legal AI model in Brazil and watched it explode—18x growth in months. But rapid scale exposed the gaps: 150+ AI agents running in production hit silent regressions, runaway tails, and privilege creep that traditional infrastructure couldn't predict. Instead of rebuilding, they extracted what worked into the AI Factory—a reusable system for domain-specific AI at scale. Learn how bounded contracts, scoped identity, and production observability transformed failure modes into design patterns. See the architecture that powers their platform and how you can apply the same m", "language": "en"},"recQYmqcMJtFF5pke": {"abstract": "Build your strategy and your cloud skills with BuilderCards- a fun, interactive deck-building game challenging you to connect AWS services and architect scalable solutions. Come share laughs, strategize together, and engage in a friendly competition, no matter where you are on your cloud journey.", "language": "en"},"recRLqOwxp3XEBy31": {"abstract": "Leading AI research labs are investing billions in competing approaches to self-improving AI. One relies on open-ended learning algorithms that let systems discover their own improvement paths. Another treats reward hacking as the central bottleneck and argues it can be solved. A third links software progress to advances in the silicon running it. This session debates which approach can sustain improvement, where each is likely to fail, and how their timelines differ. The discussion examines the tradeoffs between faster learning and reliable behavior, including when hardware co-design becomes necessary, helping teams assess which assumptions to use when planning their own AI roadmaps.", "language": "en"},"recbBLOIoDrlIRBu7": {"abstract": "Eligible attendees can collect their exclusive conference swag at designated pickup locations in Hall C of the Venetian Convention Center. Attendees must present their valid conference badge to receive their items. Swag availability is limited and distributed on a first-come, first-served basis during official event hours. Note that eligibility for items may depend on your attendee pass type.", "language": "en"},"recbCMUeaSbejIr44": {"abstract": "Clay, a Forbes AI 50 2026-listed market leader in go-to-market intelligence, helps sales teams identify promising accounts and decide how to engage them. Solange Levy, Head of AI, demonstrates how Clay combines CRM and first-party data into a context graph that guides AI agents through account research and prioritization. A product demo connects customer data to recommended sales actions, with examples from AI-native companies such as Cursor. The session examines how the emerging GTM Engineer role changes sales work and supports outbound campaigns. Founders and sales operations leaders can assess how this approach could shorten sales cycles and improve targeting of new accounts.", "language": "en"},"recbKAWRHdwoNQo22": {"abstract": "Build your strategy and your cloud skills with BuilderCards- a fun, interactive deck-building game challenging you to connect AWS services and architect scalable solutions. Come share laughs, strategize together, and engage in a friendly competition, no matter where you are on your cloud journey.", "language": "en"},"recchRtztSPZ4GKvl": {"abstract": "Get practical advice from a panel of startup founders and leaders with real world experience marketing and selling with the AWS field through the Global Startup Program. Understand what it takes to qualify, how to get discovered, build pipeline, and win enterprise customer deals with the AWS field.", "language": "en"},"rececGs8g9Whcckwz": {"abstract": "Eligible attendees can collect their exclusive conference swag at designated pickup locations in Hall C of the Venetian Convention Center. Attendees must present their valid conference badge to receive their items. Swag availability is limited and distributed on a first-come, first-served basis during official event hours. Note that eligibility for items may depend on your attendee pass type.", "language": "en"},"recg5qiHlsEyURNiE": {"abstract": "Agility Robotics’ humanoid robot, Digit, handles repetitive physical tasks, such as moving empty totes, in manufacturing environments at customers including Toyota and Amazon. The company uses Amazon SageMaker to train policy models that guide Digit’s actions and hosts Arc, its robot orchestration platform, on AWS. Rian Bogle explains the infrastructure decisions behind expanding Digit’s capabilities across customer deployments. The session examines how Agility iterates on robotic policies and coordinates robot fleets, with a demo showing Digit in action. Learn how Agility uses AWS to train robot policies and coordinate fleets that handle repetitive work in manufacturing environments.", "language": "en"},"reclM2RTPNcxe5xls": {"abstract": "A fireside chat with Sequoia Capital to explore why the choose AI-first companies with seed investments of $50 million to $75 million. Drawing on investments in cybersecurity and data infrastructure, they examine how founders demonstrate technical defensibility and a credible path to enterprise adoption. The conversation covers unit economics and go-to-market strategy, including why deployment speed matters alongside model quality. Founders raising capital will hear how these investors assess competitive advantages, evaluate business models, and judge whether customers can adopt a product quickly. The fireside chat offers insight into the evidence investors seek when evaluating ambitious AI companies.", "language": "en"}};
  window.APP_DATA.sessions.forEach(function (s) {
    var x = m[s.session_id];
    if (x) for (var k in x) if (x[k]) s[k] = x[k];
  });
})();
